      "author": {
        "id": 1
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-02-26T01:31:23Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes -- I think it\u0027s important for tenant admins to be able to selectively enable this so that users don\u0027t evade their workflow definitions.",
      "parentUuid": "00140040_38f0f82c",
      "revId": "5cec65e6ccd6bd25ec926038de2471f0f2c53eda",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "32117b0c_0c03e7ed",
        "filename": "doc/source/developer/specs/parameterized-builds.rst",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 210,
      "author": {
        "id": 4146
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-02-26T01:24:00Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think that we would also need to warn against or prevent the case of the artifact being a git commit. Specifically, you could theoretically configure a gate queue to be parameterized then supply the ref as a gerrit change ref (or the github PR equivalent), have that get tested and if the pipeline is configured to submit merge the change?\n\nIn this case I think we can prevent this from happening by disallowing allow-parameters: true and submit: true in the same pipeline definition? This weird corner case may be worth calling out explicitly simply because it has some hard to think about consequences if used this way.",
      "revId": "5cec65e6ccd6bd25ec926038de2471f0f2c53eda",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543"
